| # `convert_to_pattern` (reference/gnumake/src/rule.c:361) turns a suffix rule
| # whose target suffix is `.a` into TWO pattern rules, not one: the ordinary
| # `%.a: %.c`, and `(%.o): %.c` — the one that files an object straight into an
| # archive. This case is the second.
| #
| # The target pattern is literally `(%.o)` whatever the source suffix is, which
| # is why the stem is the member's basename and not its whole name: matching
| # `(foo.o)` binds `$*` to `foo` and `$<` to `foo.c`, where the built-in
| # `(%): %` rule would have bound them to `foo.o` and `foo.o`. That difference
| # is the whole point of the conversion, so the recipe records all four.
| .SUFFIXES:
| .SUFFIXES: .a .o .c
| .c.a:
| 	@echo "at=$@ pct=$% star=$* lt=$<" >> log
| all: libx.a(foo.o)
| 	@echo "done [$^]" >> log
